{"input_text": "Question: There is something unclear about the following example. Namely, they used symmetry in order to calculate the surface integral by using Gauss. I tried not to use symmetry, and just put that the field is F= x^3*i, and I still got the same solution. Is it okay if I do that, or must y and z be present in F?  


Answer: $\newcommand{\bbx}[1]{\,\bbox[15px,border:1px groove navy]{\displaystyle{#1}}\,}
 \newcommand{\braces}[1]{\left\lbrace\,{#1}\,\right\rbrace}
 \newcommand{\bracks}[1]{\left\lbrack\,{#1}\,\right\rbrack}
 \newcommand{\dd}{\mathrm{d}}
 \newcommand{\ds}[1]{\displaystyle{#1}}
